course: world
lesson: 5
title: The Sun as a star and the stars
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The Sun is a star: a gigantic ball of very hot gas that makes its own light and heat.
explain: The other stars are suns too, many bigger and brighter than ours, but they are so far away that they look like tiny points of light.
explain: Light from the Sun takes about eight minutes to reach us, while light from the next nearest star takes more than four years.
explain: The Sun looks bigger and brighter than other stars only because it is much closer, just as a car's headlights look huge near you and like small dots far down the road.
explain: Stars have different colours: the hottest are blue-white, our Sun is yellow-white, and cooler stars are orange or red.
fact: The Sun is a star.
fact: A star is a huge ball of hot gas that gives out its own light.
fact: The Sun is the star closest to the Earth.
fact: Light from the Sun reaches the Earth in about eight minutes.
fact: The nearest star after the Sun is more than four light-years away; a light-year is how far light travels in one year.
fact: When you look at a far star, you see light that left it long ago, so you see the star as it was when that light set off.
fact: The Sun is so big that about a million Earths could fit inside it.
fact: Many stars are bigger and brighter than the Sun.
fact: Stars look small because they are very far away.
fact: The colour of a star shows how hot it is: blue stars are hotter than red stars.
fact: Planets and the Moon do not make their own light; they reflect sunlight.
fact: Groups of stars that make pictures in the sky are called constellations.
fact: The Sun is one of billions of stars in our galaxy, the Milky Way.
fact: You must never look straight at the Sun, because it can damage your eyes.
exercise: What is the Sun? | A star | A planet | A ball of burning wood | A moon of the Earth
exercise: What is a star made of? | Very hot gas | Solid rock | Ice and dust | Liquid metal
exercise: Why does the Sun look bigger than other stars? | It is much closer to us | It is the biggest star there is | Other stars are only small sparks | It is a different kind of object
exercise: How long does sunlight take to reach the Earth? | About eight minutes | Less than one second | About one day | About one year
exercise: What is the difference between a star and a planet? | A star makes its own light; a planet reflects light | A planet makes its own light; a star reflects light | A star is always smaller than a planet | A planet is always hotter than a star
exercise: Which stars are the hottest? | Blue-white stars | Red stars | Orange stars | Yellow stars
exercise: How many Earths could fit inside the Sun? | About a million | About ten | About a hundred | About two
exercise: What is a constellation? | A group of stars that makes a picture in the sky | A star with planets going round and round it | A very bright planet that you can see at night | A cloud of gas and dust floating between the planets
exercise: What is the name of our galaxy? | The Milky Way | The Solar System | The Great Bear | The Andromeda Galaxy
exercise: Why should you never look straight at the Sun? | It can damage your eyes | It makes the Sun go dim | It makes clouds come | It makes the stars disappear
exercise: How do other stars compare with the Sun? | Many are bigger and brighter | All are smaller and dimmer | All are exactly the same | None of them give light
exercise: Why does the Moon shine at night? | It reflects the light of the Sun | It is a small star | It burns gas inside it | It catches the light of the stars
exercise: How far away is the nearest star after the Sun? | More than four light-years | About eight light-minutes | About as far as the Moon | Just past the planet Neptune
exercise: What colour is our Sun as a star? | Yellow-white | Deep blue | Dark red | Green
test: A boy says the stars are tiny lights much smaller than the Sun. What is true? | Many stars are as big as the Sun or bigger, but very far away | He is right: all stars are tiny, much smaller than the Sun | Stars are small holes in the sky that let the light shine through | Stars are pieces that broke off the Sun long ago
test: If the Sun suddenly went out, when would we notice? | About eight minutes later | At once | The next day | A year later
test: You see a red star and a blue star. Which is hotter? | The blue star | The red star | They are equally hot | You cannot tell anything from colour
test: Which is true? | The Sun is an ordinary star that is close to us | The Sun is the biggest and brightest star in the whole sky | The Sun is a planet that burns | The Sun is a star made of fire and wood
test: Venus shines brightly in the evening sky. Why does it shine? | It reflects sunlight | It is a star that makes light | It is very hot from the inside | It reflects light from the Earth
test: A street lamp near you looks bigger and brighter than one far away, though both are the same. What does this explain? | Why the Sun looks bigger and brighter than other stars | Why the Moon seems to change its shape during the month | Why we have warm summers and cold winters | Why stars seem to twinkle in the night sky
test: Which of these makes its own light? | A star | The Moon | Mars | A comet's rock
test: Stars that make the shape of a hunter in the sky are an example of what? | A constellation | A galaxy | A solar system | A planet
test: Light from a star took a hundred years to reach you. What do you see? | The star as it was a hundred years ago | The star as it is right now | The star as it will be in a hundred years | Only the reflection of the Sun
test: is the sun a star or what | Yes, it's a star, the closest one to us | No, it's a planet that is on fire | No, it's a giant moon of the Earth | No, stars are much smaller than the Sun
test: why do stars look so tiny if they're massive | Because they're very, very far away | Because they really are smaller than the Moon | Because the air shrinks them at night | Because they are much colder than the Sun
test: Leo says the Sun's the biggest star there is. true? | No, many stars are bigger and brighter than the Sun | Yes, it's the biggest and brightest star in the whole sky | Yes, that's why it looks the biggest to us | No, the Moon is a bigger star than the Sun
test: a kid in class said red stars are the hottest ones. right? | No, blue stars are hotter than red stars | Yes, red means the star is the hottest | Yes, red and blue stars are just as hot | No, white stars are always the coldest
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Sun? | It is a star, being a vast sphere of incandescent gas | It is a planet of exceptionally high temperature | It is a burning mass of solid coal | It is the largest star in existence
exam: State the approximate duration required for the light of the Sun to reach the Earth. | Approximately eight minutes | Approximately eight seconds | Approximately eight hours | Approximately eight days
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the stars appear exceedingly small to an observer upon the Earth. | Their distance from the Earth is immense | Their actual dimensions are minute | They are partially obscured by the atmosphere | They emit only feeble light
exam: It is given that one star appears blue and another red. Ascertain which inference is warranted. | The blue star possesses the higher temperature | The red star possesses the higher temperature | Both stars possess an identical temperature | The colour bears no relation to temperature
